# Framework standards

Before editing framework code: follow the router in **`framework-structure-and-skills.mdc`**, then read **`.cursor/skills/rules-md/SKILL.md`** for locations and wiring patterns. Do not guess file locations, `path.js` exports, or patterns.

---

## Always (every chat)

- **Do not commit** unless the user explicitly asks.
- **Do not run** scripts or API calls that **create / update / deactivate org data** unless the user explicitly asks to run them. Writing code ≠ permission to execute against an org.
- When the user corrects a **generic** framework mistake, **update this file** or ask if it should be saved. Script/product one-offs stay in the spec only — see **Out of scope**.
- **One-time scripts:** If the user says **one-time** / **onetimescript**, put the spec in **`tests/oneTimeScripts/`** (sibling of `tests/API/` and `tests/UI/`). Import from **`../../path.js`**. Point npm at `tests/oneTimeScripts/[name].spec.js`. Never under `tests/API/…` or `tests/UI/…`.

---

## Easy English (chat + comments)

When answering in chat or writing comments in specs/helpers/testData:

- Use short, everyday words. One clear sentence beats a long paragraph.
- Skip jargon unless the user already used that word. If you need a technical term, explain it right away in plain words.
- Do not use internal labels (e.g. probe, preflight, isolate, engine) in replies without translating them.
- Code comments: say **why** in plain English — e.g. “check skills for each job title once”, not codenames.
- Plans and lists: simple headers and short bullets. Big tables only when the user asked for numbers.

This does not change **`logger.info` / `logger.error`** style for automation runs unless the user asks.

---

## Mental model (how this repo works)

```
testData (what) → payload (body) → apibuilder (HTTP) → path.js (export)
                                      ↑
spec (orchestrates) ← imports only from path.js
npm script → package.json = Jenkins command = CSV row (same name)
```

- **Apibuilders** = shared HTTP: call, status check, log, return body.
- **Specs** = business rules, filtering, orchestration, testData.
- **`path.js`** = only import surface for specs/helpers.

---

## Simple logic and readable code

### Prefer clear branches over clever overloads

- If behavior depends on an obvious signal (e.g. search **has `@`** vs not), use **two paths**, not one function that tries every trick.
- **One responsibility per small function.**
- Avoid unsafe shortcuts (e.g. “if exactly one result, return it”) unless the requirement is explicit.

### Variable names

- Names state meaning: `searchHasAt`, `usersFromResponse`, `rowEmail` — not `q`, `x`, `data2`.
- Booleans: **`is…` / `has…` / `should…`**.
- Avoid single-letter loop variables in non-trivial loops.
- Say **what the value is**, not how it was built. A reader should guess the shape from the name.

**Quick patterns (this repo)**

| Kind of value | Name pattern | Examples |
|---------------|--------------|----------|
| List / array | plural noun | `roleNames`, `eligiblePoolLearners`, `chatPeople` |
| Map / lookup | `…By…` or `…For…` | `skillCountsByRole`, `skillsByName`, `tokensByLogin` |
| Count / number | what you counted + `Count` | `goalsCreated`, `userCount`, `completeTaskCount` |
| One person row | `person`, `learner`, `orgUser`, or `loginName` in logs | not `item`, `row`, `u` in non-trivial code |
| Filtered subset | name the filter | `rolesForPool`, `eligibleForSeed`, `foundDefaultUsers` |
| Spare / helper account | say the role in the flow | `spareLearner` (skill check only), not `probe`, `testUser` |
| Log string | what the reader will see | `poolRolesWithSkillCounts`, not `usedWithCounts`, `summaryText` |
| API body | `…Body` or product term | `dataBody`, `assessmentBody`, not `res`, `json` |

| Avoid | Prefer | Why |
|--------|--------|-----|
| `usedWithCounts`, `data2`, `temp` | `poolRolesWithSkillCounts` | Content is roles + counts |
| `result`, `output`, `info` | `skillCountsByRole`, `pickedLearners` | Says what came back from the step |
| `x`, `arr`, `obj` | `roleNames`, `learnersWithTokens` | Full words |
| `item` in a batch loop over people | `person`, `learner` | `for (let person of labeled)` |
| `n`, `i` when meaning is known | `goalIndex`, `batchIndex`, `mixIndex` | OK for tiny inner loops; not for business steps |
| `check`, `handle`, `process` verbs as nouns | `discoverRoleSkillCounts`, `ensureProfileAndAssessment` | Functions can be verbs; variables should be nouns |
| `roles`, `users` when type matters | `allPoolRoles`, `orgUsers`, `poolEligible` | Which list of roles/users |

**Spec / seed examples**

```javascript
const defaultLoginSet = defaultLoginNamesSet();
const eligiblePoolLearners = orgUsers.filter((user) => isPoolLearner(user, defaultLoginSet));
const shuffledEligible = reusableFunctions.shuffleDataInArray(eligiblePoolLearners);
const spareLearner = (await collectLearnersWithTokens(request, shuffledEligible, 1))[0];
const eligibleForSeed = shuffledEligible.filter((user) => user.loginName !== spareLearner.loginName);
const pickedLearners = await collectLearnersWithTokens(request, eligibleForSeed, userCount);
const skillCountsByRole = await discoverRoleSkillCounts(request, spareLearner, allPoolRoles);
const rolesForPool = rolesWithSkills(skillCountsByRole, allPoolRoles);
const poolRolesWithSkillCounts = rolesForPool
  .map((role) => `${role} (${skillCountsByRole.get(role) || 0})`)
  .join(", ");
```

**Search / user lookup examples**

```javascript
const searchHasAt = searchLower.includes("@");
const usersFromResponse = body?.users || [];
const rowEmail = (user.email || "").toLowerCase();
const hasCompletedProfileAndAssessment =
  dataBody?.is_profile_completed === true && dataBody?.self_assessment_is_completed === true;
```

**Apibuilder return (in spec — name the slice you use)**

```javascript
const goalId = created?.goalId;
const usersFromOrg = (await getAllUserApi(request, jwt))?.users || [];
// not: const data = await …; then data.users everywhere without a named pull
```

### Readability

- Happy path first: normalize → branch → loop.
- Non-obvious condition → one-line comment on **why**, not what the next line does.

```javascript
let searchHasAt = searchLower.includes("@");
for (let user of users) {
  if (searchHasAt) {
    if (emailFieldsMatch(user, searchLower)) return user;
  } else {
    if (firstLastMatches(user, searchLower)) return user;
  }
}
```

