## Shared API builders — no spec-specific behavior

**Do not** change shared apibuilder signatures, defaults, filtering, or return shape for one caller.

**Do:** call as documented; filter/map/validate in the **spec** (or a spec-local helper). For a different raw call, small spec helper using **`urlData` / `headerData` / payloads from `path.js`** — do not fork the shared apibuilder.

An apibuilder file only exports the API functions. Do not add other `async` or sync functions in that file. Extra logic belongs in the spec.

Do not add spec-only helpers to shared files (e.g. `responseValidation.js`) unless truly shared by multiple specs.

---

## Imports — one statement from `path.js` only

- Never import from `helpers/API/...` directly in specs/apibuilders.
- One destructured `import { ... } from "../../../path.js"` (correct relative depth; oneTimeScripts → `../../path.js`).
- Do not split into multiple import lines from different paths.
- Do not reformat an existing single-line import to multi-line unless the user asks.
- New symbol → export from **`path.js` first**, then import.

---

## Comments on helpers and sections

- Section markers: `// ===================== Helper Functions =====================`
- One-line comment above every helper: **why** / business rule — not “what the next line does.”
- Match RAS/Admin style (e.g. `riskAndReadinessTimeliness.spec.js`).
- Comments stay **plain and easy**. Say why in everyday words. Do not overcomplicate or add jargon. Write only for the line or helper in front of you — do not reuse wording from another feature.

---

## Keep code simple

- Match the **simplest existing spec** in the same module folder.
- Reuse apibuilders and **`reusableFunctions`** before writing new helpers.
- Generic helpers (dates, uuid) belong on **`reusableFunctions`**. Do not copy them into a spec.
- Search the repo before inventing a parallel implementation.
- Prefer small `if / else` or spec-local helper over new shared abstractions.
- Do not create extra files for one concern — extend existing pieces.
- **Tags / `--grep`:** only when one spec has multiple selectable tests and npm must run a subset. Single-test specs → no `@tag` / `--grep`.

---

## No duplicate or dead code

- Check the framework already has the helper / payload field / npm command before adding.
- Remove unused imports and dead helpers when touching an area.
- Do not copy-paste payload blocks — use fields you actually need.

---

## Logging — human-readable only

- **`logger.info()`** / **`logger.error()`** — short plain English.
- Do not log raw objects, full API responses, or debug blobs unless the user asks.
- Do not double-log the same success/failure.
- **No `console.log`.**

### Choosing the level — `error` means something broke

**`logger.error()` — only when the code or an API actually failed.** Someone reading the log must be able to treat every ERROR line as a thing to go fix.

- The HTTP status check failed (inside the `catch`, in the apibuilder).
- The call threw: network error, timeout, bad JSON.
- The script cannot continue and is about to throw.

**`logger.info()` — everything else, including every planned skip.** A skip is not a failure. If the run did exactly what it was designed to do, it is INFO even though a learner got no data.

- Why a user was skipped — not in this org, no token, no matching testData.
- Why a step was not done — role has no skills in the catalog, no goal created so no chat, group is meant to stop early.
- An API returned 200 but the body had nothing usable (empty list, missing id). The call worked; the **data** was not there.
- Retrying, falling back to another value, progress, batch and summary lines.

### Rules that follow from this

- **Log the failure once, at the source.** The apibuilder's `catch` owns the ERROR line. A caller that reacts to it (skips the user, picks another, retries) writes **INFO** describing what it did instead. Do not write ERROR in both places.
- **Say why, and what happened to that user.** `logger.error("failed")` and `logger.info("skipped")` are useless. Name the person, the step, and the reason: `Alex_7 | group A | Project Manager has no skills in this org's AI Coach catalog — skipped goal 1`.
- **Write for someone who does not know the feature.** No internal flag names, no status-code dumps, no stack traces.
- **A clean run should have zero ERROR lines.** Before finishing, re-read every `logger.error` you added and ask: did something break here, or did the script just choose not to do something? If it chose, it is INFO.

